PostGIS's tests under ORCA: the differences from PostGIS's expected output
that have been reviewed, and are ORCA's plans rather than wrong answers.

Each file is a test's difference in the form ../../singlenode/canon.pl gives
it -- the lines removed and added, hunk by hunk, with no file names or line
numbers -- under the test's own path.  run.sh counts a test as passing when
its difference is exactly the file's; any other difference fails it, and is
left for review.  None of these tests differs in the planner pass.

A test inspects its own plan (7)

    regress/core/regress_brin_index
    regress/core/regress_brin_index_3d
    regress/core/regress_brin_index_geography
    regress/core/regress_index
    regress/core/regress_spgist_index_2d
    regress/core/regress_spgist_index_3d
    regress/core/regress_spgist_index_nd

  Each sets enable_seqscan, enable_indexscan and enable_bitmapscan, reads the
  names of the scans back from EXPLAIN, and prints them.  ORCA does not read
  PostgreSQL's enable_* settings, and plans no SP-GiST index scan at all (it
  is not an access method ORCA's translator lets through), so the scan named
  is ORCA's choice.

  Where the scan differs, two of them print different rows as well, and the
  rows are PostgreSQL's own: regress_brin_index_3d's query
  'BOX(125 125,126 126)'::box2d ~ the_geom returns eighteen POINT Z EMPTY
  rows by a seq scan and one by a BRIN bitmap scan under PostgreSQL's planner
  too, and the test expects each answer where it forces each scan.  ORCA
  chose the bitmap scan where the test forces a seq scan, and answered what
  the planner answers by that scan.  regress_index's selectivity checks
  ("&&|1|5+-5") compare the planner's estimate with the rows; ORCA's is not
  PostGIS's, whose selectivity functions it does not call.

A subquery's ORDER BY, and no other (4)

    regress/core/tickets                 (#680)
    topology/test/regress/st_addedgemodface
    topology/test/regress/st_addedgenewfaces
    raster/test/regress/rt_gdalwarp

  ORCA removes an ORDER BY in a subquery that has no LIMIT of its own
  (CExpressionPreprocessor::PexprRemoveSuperfluousLimit), as the SQL standard
  lets it and Cloudberry's ORCA does.  #680 takes the first row of such a
  subquery with a LIMIT outside it; the others print a GROUP BY or a select
  list over one with no ORDER BY of their own.  The rows are the same, in
  another order, or another first row.

No ORDER BY at all (1)

    raster/test/regress/rt_clip

  Its metadata query has no ORDER BY, and prints raster_clip_out in the
  order the test's INSERT ... SELECT wrote it, which ORCA planned with the
  joins in another order.  The rows are the same.

The order PostGIS's own queries return rows in (1)

    topology/test/regress/st_createtopogeo

  T14 reports the face an isolated node lies in.  ST_CreateTopoGeo assigns
  face ids in the order its own queries, which ORCA plans, return the
  geometry's parts; the nodes, edges and faces it counts are the same ("22
  nodes|24 edges|9 faces"), and the face holding the node is 5 rather than 7.

A NOTICE or WARNING from a function called more often (5)

    regress/core/estimatedextent
    raster/test/regress/rt_summarystats
    raster/test/regress/rt_mapalgebraexpr
    raster/test/regress/rt_mapalgebrafct
    raster/test/regress/rt_mapalgebrafctngb

  estimatedextent's queries read ST_EstimatedExtent(...) from a subquery
  with OFFSET 0 four times, and ORCA, which takes no OFFSET 0 for a fence,
  evaluates the STABLE function for each reference: four "stats do not
  exist" warnings where the planner has one.

  The raster tests call an IMMUTABLE function with constant arguments, which
  the port's preprocessing in front of ORCA folds -- calling it, and raising
  its NOTICE -- before ORCA declines the query for a reason of its own ("SIRV
  functions", "Row-type variable"); the planner then plans the query from
  the start and folds the call again.  Cloudberry's fold_constants runs in
  the same place and has the same effect.
